Yes. The main bottlenecks in the previous version are:

1. **Reading the entire file to calculate SHA-256 before processing.**
2. **Reparsing all previously committed records after a restart.**
3. **Listing and sorting the entire directory in memory.**
4. **Repeatedly scanning completed files.**
5. **Creating JSON strings and then converting them to bytes again.**
6. **Processing only one file at a time across the whole deployment.**

I would fix the first five before adding concurrency.

**Keep the correctness mechanism unchanged: events and their checkpoint must remain in the same Kafka transaction.** Also, do not run concurrent transactions through one producer; a producer supports only one open transaction at a time. ([kafka.apache.org](https://kafka.apache.org/41/javadoc/org/apache/kafka/clients/producer/KafkaProducer.html))

Below are the changes to the previous implementation.

---

# 1. Store a byte offset, not just a record number

Instead of restarting from the beginning and skipping millions of rows, save:

```json
{
  "version": 2,
  "sourceId": "orders-directory",
  "fileName": "orders-000123.csv",
  "fileSize": 9876543210,
  "lastModifiedMillis": 1791720000000,
  "headers": ["order_id", "customer", "amount"],
  "nextRecord": 5000000,
  "nextByteOffset": 7123456789,
  "completed": false
}
```

On recovery:

```java
channel.position(checkpoint.nextByteOffset());
```

**Important:** the offset must be the beginning of the next CSV record—not `FileChannel.position()` after parsing. Buffered readers read ahead, and physical newline boundaries are not necessarily CSV-record boundaries.

Commons CSV exposes each record’s starting byte position when byte tracking is enabled. We will use that position with **one-record lookahead**. ([commons.apache.org](https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-csv/apidocs/org/apache/commons/csv/CSVRecord.html?utm_source=openai))

This avoids rereading the committed file prefix. It does not eliminate Kafka checkpoint-recovery time.

### Replace `Checkpoint.java`

```java
package com.example.ingest;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;

public record Checkpoint(
        int version,
        String sourceId,
        String fileName,
        long fileSize,
        long lastModifiedMillis,
        List<String> headers,
        long nextRecord,
        long nextByteOffset,
        boolean completed
) {
    public Checkpoint {
        if (version != 2) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "Unsupported checkpoint version; migration required"
            );
        }

        if (sourceId == null || sourceId.isBlank()
                || fileName == null || fileName.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Missing source/file identity");
        }

        if (fileSize < 0
                || nextRecord < 0
                || nextByteOffset < 0
                || nextByteOffset > fileSize) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid checkpoint position");
        }

        if (completed && nextByteOffset != fileSize) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "Completed checkpoint must point to EOF"
            );
        }

        headers = List.copyOf(headers);

        if (headers.isEmpty()
                || headers.stream().anyMatch(String::isBlank)
                || new HashSet<>(headers).size() != headers.size()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid CSV headers");
        }
    }
}
```

### File identity trade-off

Remove full-file hashing from the ingestion hot path.

The code below checks size and modification time, but **these are change detectors, not cryptographic identity checks**. This optimization relies on the original contract:

- Files are immutable after publication.
- Filenames are never reused.
- Publication happens through an atomic rename.

If you need a cryptographic fingerprint, have the publisher calculate it while writing the file and publish it in a trusted manifest. Do not add an extra full-file read before every restart.

