# 6. Stream directory entries and archive completed files

Replace the previous:

```java
Files.list(directory)
        .sorted()
        .toList();
```

with a `DirectoryStream`. This avoids building and sorting a list of every filename. Java exposes directory streams for iterating directory entries without materializing them all into a collection. ([docs.oracle.com](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html))

Inside `scan()`, after initialization and `verifyUnfinishedFilesExist()`:

```java
try (var entries = Files.newDirectoryStream(
        config.directory(), "*.csv")) {

    for (Path file : entries) {
        if (stopping) {
            return;
        }

        if (!Files.isRegularFile(file, LinkOption.NOFOLLOW_LINKS)) {
            continue;
        }

        String fileName = file.getFileName().toString();
        Checkpoint checkpoint = journal.checkpoint(fileName);

        if (checkpoint == null || !checkpoint.completed()) {
            processFile(file, checkpoint);
        }

        Checkpoint committed = journal.checkpoint(fileName);

        if (committed != null && committed.completed()) {
            // processFile has returned and its reader/channel are closed.
            archiveCompleted(file);
        }
    }
}
```

Add:

```java
private void archiveCompleted(Path file) throws IOException {
    Path target = config.archiveDirectory()
            .resolve(file.getFileName());

    if (Files.exists(target, LinkOption.NOFOLLOW_LINKS)) {
        throw new IOException(
                "Archive destination already exists: " + target
        );
    }

    Files.move(
            file,
            target,
            java.nio.file.StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE
    );
}
```

Use a dedicated archive directory with no competing writers. `ATOMIC_MOVE` support depends on the filesystem/provider; configure the directories on the same filesystem and fail rather than silently falling back to copying multi-gigabyte files. ([docs.oracle.com](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html))

### The ordering is important

```text
Commit final records + completed checkpoint
                    ↓
Close CSV reader
                    ↓
Move file to archive
```

The move is **not** part of the Kafka transaction. That is safe here because it is cleanup:

- Crash before final commit: resume from the previous checkpoint.
- Crash after commit, before move: recognize the completed checkpoint and move the file without republishing it.
- Crash after move: the completed checkpoint remains authoritative.

**Never move or delete an unfinished file.**

The archive should have its own retention policy. Do not delete its Kafka completion checkpoint merely because the source file was archived.

