# 8. Remaining large-scale limits

These patches substantially change file I/O behavior, but they do not solve every scaling issue.

| Area | After these changes |
|---|---|
| Resuming a large file | Seek directly to the next uncommitted record |
| Full-file hash pass | Removed from ingestion |
| Filename enumeration | Streaming, unsorted |
| Completed files in incoming directory | Archived after commit |
| Event memory | Bounded batch plus parser/producer overhead |
| File concurrency | Configurable through independent shards |
| Checkpoint index | Still retains one entry per historical file |
| Kafka checkpoint recovery | Still replays the compacted checkpoint topic |

If you have **millions of historical files**, replace the in-memory `checkpoints` map with a disk-backed index. Kafka should remain authoritative; any local index/snapshot must be rebuilt or caught up through the committed recovery boundary before ingestion starts.

Also:

- A filesystem watcher can reduce discovery latency, but retain reconciliation scans. Java watch services can overflow or have implementation-specific limitations, particularly on remote filesystems. ([docs.oracle.com](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/WatchService.html))
- Do not split large CSV files at arbitrary newlines. Prefer having the publisher produce smaller independently valid CSV files if a single huge file becomes the parallelism bottleneck.
- Retain parser/input limits. Bounding serialized event size does not stop the parser from allocating a huge malformed field.

## Migration warning

**Do not deploy the version-2 checkpoint model over existing version-1 checkpoints without migration. Do not delete the checkpoint topic to “start fresh.”**

For an existing deployment, stop the old writer and perform a one-time migration:

1. Recover the old committed checkpoint.
2. Verify the old file hash.
3. Parse the header and skip the old `nextRecord` once.
4. Determine the next record’s byte offset.
5. Commit the version-2 checkpoint without emitting events.

Preserve the source identity and transactional ownership during migration. Also note that the example output is now `schemaVersion: 2` and no longer contains `fileSha256`.

**My recommended order: byte-offset recovery → byte-array serialization → streaming discovery/archive → benchmark batching → add stable shards.** This removes unnecessary work before introducing concurrency.